※ pl4g-test: compile=error diag=4570

※ A lambda reaches what it brought in by reference, so it lasts no longer than
※ the shortest-lived of those.  Giving one to a name that outlives them is the
※ same escape a bare reference is refused for, and calling it afterwards would
※ read a variable the program can no longer name.
※
※ What is brought in by *value* ties the lambda to nothing: a name standing for
※ a place hands over what is at the place, so what the lambda holds is a copy.

let b1 : bool = true

@[startup, impure]
fn main() → u6:
    let v1 : mut u32 = 5u32
    let f : mut fn() → u32 = λ [&v1] → u32 { v1 }
    if b1:
        let v2 : mut u32 = 9u32
        f ← λ [&v2] → u32 { v2 }
    if f() = 5u32 { 0u6 } else { 1u6 }
